 <div class="card" id="help-malware"><h2>Malware / Quarantine</h2><p>Quarantine moves a file out of place into a private vault, keeps it from running, and records where it came from. Enter an absolute path to scan one file with ClamAV; an infected file is quarantined (tick <b>Quarantine even if clean</b> to quarantine a file ClamAV does not flag). Infected files found by scheduled scans are listed under <b>Detections awaiting action</b> with a <b>Quarantine</b> button each. In <b>Quarantined files</b>, <b>Restore</b> puts a file back exactly where it was, with its original owner and permissions (setuid/setgid bits are not restored), and <b>Delete</b> removes it permanently; <b>Delete all</b> empties the vault. A file whose quarantine record is missing (an interrupted quarantine) has no recorded location, so it can only be deleted. Paths under core system directories (<code>/bin</code>, <code>/usr/lib</code>, <code>/etc</code> and similar) are refused unless you tick <b>Force</b>, so a false positive cannot disable a working system binary.</p><p><a href="#help-top">Back to contents</a></p></div>
 
-<div class="card" id="help-threats"><h2>Threat Intelligence</h2><p>Scanistry keeps a local copy of public feeds (Spamhaus DROP, Feodo Tracker and URLhaus by default, set in <code>feed_urls</code>) and refreshes it every <code>feed_interval</code> seconds (12 hours). Each feed is capped at 8 MB on download and the stored indicator list at 200,000 entries per category, so a feed that suddenly publishes millions of entries cannot grow the daemon's memory or the state directory without limit; the page shows counts rather than downloading the indicator list itself. <b>Check reputation</b> searches that local copy only; nothing you enter is sent anywhere. With <code>feed_auto_block</code> on, feed addresses are blocked automatically, at most <code>feed_auto_block_limit</code> per refresh.</p><p>If an update reaches none of the sources (no network or DNS), the previous indicators are kept, the page says the last attempt failed, and the update is retried every 15 minutes until it succeeds. <b>Last updated</b> is the time of the last successful update.</p><p><a href="#help-top">Back to contents</a></p></div>
+<div class="card" id="help-threats"><h2>Threat Intelligence</h2><p>Scanistry keeps a local copy of public feeds (Spamhaus DROP and Feodo Tracker by default, set in <code>feed_urls</code>, plus URLhaus when <code>urlhaus_auth_key</code> is set) and refreshes it every <code>feed_interval</code> seconds (12 hours). Each feed is capped at 8 MB on download and the stored indicator list at 200,000 entries per category, so a feed that suddenly publishes millions of entries cannot grow the daemon's memory or the state directory without limit; the page shows counts rather than downloading the indicator list itself. <b>Check reputation</b> searches that local copy only; nothing you enter is sent anywhere. With <code>feed_auto_block</code> on, feed addresses are blocked automatically, at most <code>feed_auto_block_limit</code> per refresh.</p><p>If an update reaches none of the sources (no network or DNS), the previous indicators are kept, the page says the last attempt failed, and the update is retried every 15 minutes until it succeeds. <b>Last updated</b> is the time of the last successful update.</p><p><b>URLhaus</b> (malware download sites) has needed a free abuse.ch Auth-Key since 2026-10-23; its old addresses answer HTTP 410 Gone. To use it, sign in at https://auth.abuse.ch/, generate an Auth-Key, put it in <code>"urlhaus_auth_key"</code> in /etc/scanistry/scanistry.conf and run <code>sudo systemctl restart scanistry</code>. Scanistry then downloads the keyed export and keeps the URLs it lists as online. The key is never written to the log or shown here. Without a key the other lists work as before; a list that fails with HTTP 404 or 410 has moved or closed, and retrying will not bring it back.</p><p><a href="#help-top">Back to contents</a></p></div>
 
 <div class="card" id="help-network"><h2>Ports &amp; Capture</h2><p>Live connections and listening ports come from <code>ss</code>. <b>Start capture</b> runs tcpdump for 5 to 3600 seconds and saves the capture under <code>/var/lib/scanistry/captures/</code>. Before 4.0.11-62 captures could come out missing or empty on Debian, whose tcpdump drops to its own <code>tcpdump</code> user and could not write into that root-only directory; Scanistry now opens the file itself and tcpdump writes into it. Saved captures are listed underneath with when they were recorded and their size. <b>Analyze</b> shows who talked to whom and on which port, busiest first, with the protocol breakdown folded away underneath. Since 4.0.11-88 it uses Scanistry's own built-in reader, which looks only at the link, IP and TCP/UDP headers. It is memory-safe, checks every length, and stops after 1 GB, 3 million packets or 60 seconds. A crafted capture can therefore at worst be rejected as corrupt, and Wireshark is not involved. <b>Deep analysis (Wireshark)</b>, under the results, decodes every protocol with tshark. Wireshark's decoders have a long history of security bugs, and the packets in a capture were chosen by whoever sent them. So tshark runs as a throwaway unprivileged user (systemd DynamicUser) with no network, a read-only system, Scanistry's state, logs and keys hidden, a system-call filter and a 512 MB memory cap. It never opens the capture itself: Scanistry passes it in on standard input. Set <code>capture_deep_analysis</code> to <code>false</code> in the configuration to remove the option entirely; <b>Delete</b> removes one capture and its results, <b>Delete all captures</b> removes every one (a capture still recording is stopped first). The filename box is there for a capture that is not in the list. Captures will not start with less than 1 GB free, stop early below 256 MB, and at most 3 run at once.</p><p><a href="#help-top">Back to contents</a></p></div>
 
